ities and sifa represents an  opportunity for communication between  many different voices  this is not just the way to build tools  that are most beneficial for society but  it's also the way to drive breakthroughs  in ai itself  so areas of cutting-edge ai research  like hybrid physical models  and interpretability are spurred on  by the needs of end stakeholders to  incorporate  known system constraints in this case or  to better understand  how the algorithm is working these are  the needs and that leads to innovation  so the right kind of research ecosystem  really benefits  everybody that's great thank you niddy  mindy what about you why did you choose  edmonton and amy  well actually i'm originally from  edmonton  and i was away for many years this is my  first faculty position but i've been  working for  about 18 19 years in industry research  and most of it about 17 of those years  in france  so this was a great opportunity for me  to not only come back  to canada but to edmonton and  to participate in this wonderful  ecosystem we have here  you know i echo what david um himself  said here  about um cfar uh  having giving us this opportunity to  make these short connections between  different parts of the country  um through amy mila and vector  you know which gives us you know a wide  range of people we can collaborate with  and topics like ma the one that i'm  working on  um you know it's really important to  connect with um  you know different disciplines uh  researchers  you know privacy impacts health for  example um  and it impacts um you know other  services like recommender systems so  um this is a great opportunity to be  able to connect with people of different  disciplines  and being in this academic environment  i'm also able to connect with  other disciplines like law and sociology  and business for example where we are  starting to see a lot of impacts of ai  as well  absolutely that's again that that  multi-disciplinary  uh collaboration is really what's key to  to driving these technologies and making  sure that they're implemented in a way  that's really  responsible and effective and so while  we're talking about  responsible ai and that's a phrase that  we we throw around a lot  uh in canada you heard it quite a bit  this morning from um  speakers um rahul moe let's talk about  your areas of application um you know  what are some of the important  considerations  that you think about or that the  community thinks about  in health or in robotics when it comes  to ensuring  that your ml approaches are implemented  in a way that is responsible  how about we'll start with raul uh  absolutely i think the area of  healthcare  is one where you cannot blindly walk in  and make the assumption that any black  box machine learning  algorithm will perform well and so this  is particularly true for two reasons  one there exists societal biases  that manifest in the data that what  these algorithms are trained on  and the naive application of these  algorithms  might simply amplify the existing biases  two  knowing what is relevant for the  prediction task at hand  is often not something that  most machine learning practitioners will  be well burst about  and furthermore what is relevant might  simply change  across the different context that the  model may be considered to be applied in  so what's relevant in hospital one for  predicting a patient  outcome may simply not be relevant in  hospital too because the way they do  things in terms of their processes is  fundamentally different  so i think in the context of healthcare  uh there's two key considerations  for uh thinking about responsible ai  that  i think are important to start with at  least and there's many many more  and so the first is working with domain  experts  i think there's absolutely no substitute  for working with a domain expert in  an area like healthcare because uh  he or she is the one who's worked with  the data  uh every day of their career and they  arguably  know about any limitations that the data  may have  and can think through the impacts of  those limitations on the downstream  performance  of any model that's being developed and  so the second key consideration i'd say  is monitoring the performance of the  model in any deployed solution  so um i think you know this notion of  responsible ai is not  uh it's not a one-off it doesn't stop  when the model  is done training it's uh sort of a  commitment to continue to see  how the model performs uh in a live  scenario  and moderate monitor it for unintended  consequences  and what i want to point out here is  that these unintended consequences  need not be from what the model is  predicting alone  they could be much bigger uh implied  consequences that come about  as the model is used in practice so to  ground that in a concrete example  you can think of a prediction tool that  say predicts whether or not a patient  should be  recommended for a second opinion uh for  in the context of say  a disease and so if this model is really  really  good at this prediction task what  essentially happens is that you have a  much larger volume of people  who are now going to doctors and  specialists for second opinions but we  have a finite set of resources  or a finite set of specialists who may  not have the capacity to handle that  volume  and so this is an unintended consequence  not due to the failure of the model but  rather due to the systemic nature  of the healthcare system that this model  is deployed in  and so i i'd say that like it's  important to think about both the micro  and the macro scale consequences  of deployed solutions and machine  learning in this context yeah absolutely  and and  both of those examples that you gave  remind me of especially in a healthcare  context this idea of you know the humans  in the loop right like so keeping the  clinicians very close to  the development of the machine learning  algorithms and tools that are being  deployed  but then uh you know we as as  practitioners  following uh the evolution of our models  as they are deployed  and out there in the wild so that's  that's great how about umo  tell us about um you know the the  considerations around responsible ai  when we're talking about robotics yeah  in fact i mean even though health and  robotics they sound like  kind of different areas i actually think  that there's a lot in common so one of  the things that we really worry about is  like if an ai makes a error in judgment  it's not just  some misclassified image but in both  robotics and health  there's i mean there's immediate  physical consequences  um to people uh so of course i think  safety and sort of the  reliability of these systems is really  really important  and i think it's actually one of the  bottlenecks that we need to get through  in order to kind of advance the the  field of robotics in  everyday life um i think a lot of these  key words i'm going to mention you  probably have heard like  interpretability right i think  robots will make mistakes and hopefully  not too many but they will make some  mistakes and when they do we need to  understand why  and systematically kind of um uh sort of  improve the areas that they're weak at  um i think one  one thing that i am pretty interested in  is uh  to have robots realize when they should  be unsure of themselves  so um so if you have like a if you have  a driver let's say a human driver or a  human  a doctor right especially during  training they  well training is now kind of an  overloaded term now  uh so what people know that people know  when they should be unsure and maybe  when they should ask help  right this is something that i don't  really think robots are can do yet and  hopefully they will be able to do this  later  um one thing that is also very important  for robotics is uh  how can we learn safely right one of the  ways of learning safely  is in simulation and so can we develop  methods  that can robustly transfer behavior  learn in simulation to the real world so  this is a very active area of research  um so in the end i think that one of the  really  more complex things that i could i want  to consider is really people's trust  um so suppose we make these systems  very technically robust uh technically  speaking they're very very  let's say safe will people still trust  them right  a lot of people a lot of times we we've  seen uh user studies where the robot is  behaving optimally but people  feel that feel that the robot is acting  weird and even though the robot is doing  probably better than humans humans don't  necessarily trust the robots  so in terms of trust i think it's a very  very difficult topic  um i think one thing that we we have to  do  as a community is to make sure we  educate everyone right not just the  students but  the entire community including people  who have already been working in ai  right we need to understand that we can  choose to be  we can choose to trust ai or maybe be  skeptical but  but the trust or skepticism it needs to  be informed  and and i think it's a as a community  this is a very important thing  in the long term and any any thoughts mo  on  on how we should do that you know you're  talking specifically i think about  about the research community i i and my  colleagues at seafarer have been  thinking about  the public and engaging the public  around ai do you think we have to  to do both um  well obviously i'm kind of focused more  on the education of students like the  graduate students as well as the  undergraduates  just just kind of running into the maybe  like  advice for students i think uh go ahead  i would say like we need to learn the  fundamentals properly there are  no shortcuts right so a lot of times  when we think of ai we think of  perhaps downloading some toolbox that is  that makes doing research convenient but  those those should be more like a  jump start right uh there's still no  shortcut in learning the math  learning the fundamentals um and canada  being one of the  places where we really value fundamental  research this is especially important  um and i would say this extends not uh  beyond students uh this is also for  everyone involved i i've talked to many  of my friends who are actually  well educated and they are aware of ai  but i would say that  in general i really hope to see people  understand the fundamentals fundamentals  a little bit more  great yeah maybe more events like this  in fact  that that could help yeah that's that's  that's part of why we do it for sure  absolutely  okay i'm going to take a few questions  uh from the audience now we've had lots  of great questions come in  and i want to share one with you that's  uh particularly been upvoted  by a number of individuals and and uh  people uh  putting their thumbs up for this one  it's uh it's coming in from joshua so  it's  you know it's a loaded question uh what  should happen to make sure  ai for social good applications are  developed even when the profit motive  is not sufficient to motivate companies  so you know how do we make sure  that you know ai for goods applications  are  really a priority for us as a research  community a research and innovation  community  when the private sector and their profit  motive is not  uh is not high enough for them to be  involved thoughts on  on that balance anybody want to join up  i'd like to start by saying it helps to  have a monetary incentive  obviously but when that isn't there  i think in the research community can  fairly easily be convinced that this is  an important problem to look at  i think the community that needs to be  convinced is the industry the business  and the general public um sometimes  they're just not aware  um and and possibly even i mean i i  believe in a multi-pronged approach so  possibly even  sort of the regulatory or policy bodies  um  one um you know analogy i like to use  is you know industries like telecom  where i  worked for many years ago have standards  um  that their protocols must abide by and  that's important for the communication  but for areas where you don't need that  you still have certain  guidelines like many years ago the  consumer protection  agencies were very important and their  work led to things like having labels on  clothing irons that say don't iron while  you're wearing your clothes  so um and you know no one's saying that  clothing irons shouldn't be produced and  shouldn't exist  but the harm that can be produced from  it was recognized  so in the same way i think for ai  products and services  i think we all agree that they're very  important and impactful  but the harm that they may result in  is not properly recognized and so maybe  you know we need something like  something like these consumer protection  agencies  um that allow people to be educated  about the harms  and how they can be safeguarded so if  the people are then  aware of this then they can push on  using their  you know where they spend their money on  they can push the industry or businesses  in certain directions  yeah absolutely i'm reminded uh again to  go back to healthcare where it's you  know a highly regulated  sector in canada and the us and both the  fda and health canada  you know actively and carefully examine  machine learning applications uh very  closely before they're implemented  within  you know within health systems and and  they are it's happening  um and so that's that's a step in the  right direction anything else on um  on public kind of private sector go  ahead dave i can i can i can step in a  bit  so i think that one component is  using our voices not necessarily in a  research capacity  but in the capacity of thought leaders  to add to the conversation about  what best policies and best practices  are um we have  we have leadership roles that don't  involve  necessarily coming up with a new  algorithm people  it is important for the community to use  its voice in that way  and then i think another component of  this  and and then that does involve informing  stakeholders of what the capabilities of  systems are and what they can do to best  incentivize  incentivize meaningful impact  and that also means informing ourselves  what what that means  but i think another component is  remembering  that research needs to be scoped with  impact in mind from the get-go it's not  a question of i wrote a great paper with  a great project and now i'm going to  look for a way to make it impactful oh  wait  i don't see a path to impact this is why  one should work with stakeholders from  from like the initial scoping of a  project and the development of the  project in addition to the deployment of  the project because once you get to  deployment  if you haven't scoped it together with  the people who will ultimately be using  it it's too late  there are ton of opportunities where the  incentives are aligned right now and  making sure that one is working with the  people who will be deploying  your projects can help you ensure that  you are working in a space  where you will be able to have the  impact that you want  that's great david thank you for that  and it is actually your  response speaks to one of the other  questions that came in  uh that that reads what do you see as  the role of individual researchers  versus the organization or external  players like governments  ensuring that ai is used in socially  beneficial ways so i want to  sort of um share that one with the other  members of the panel  what do you see as your role uh as  individual researchers  in you know being a voice around uh  responsible use of ai anybody  yeah i can i can chime in on this i  think  as uh individual researchers um  a we serve two roles one is education  which is  educating students undergraduate at the  graduate level  and teaching them to  create new algorithms and machine  learning in robotics and health