eas are  rediscovered and reused in different  subfields and being able to flexibly  move within fields  and describe how one idea relates to  another  in a completely separate arena it will  only strengthen  uh sort of the mental models you have of  the world and ultimately  lead to more interesting kinds of  research ideas great great advice david  last word to you  yeah so to echo what other people have  said  learn the fundamentals but not just  machine learning  learn the fundamentals of whatever areas  you're looking to integrate with machine  learning  and go in with humility recognize that  each of these other areas of application  has  decades or centuries of great work in it  and machine learning is not suddenly  going to solve all those problems  um or if it does solve some of those  problems  it's going to do that only by  integrating with all of that existing  knowledge  and by really carefully working with  people who are expert in in  those areas and if you want to have an  impact start from the problem  whether it's a theoretical or an applied  problem start from the problem  and let the tools that you use  evolve to match the problem don't go in  assuming that the  fanciest newest tools are best and then  paradoxically often that is actually  what will lead to foundational  innovation  that is what will lead to the  breakthroughs in from an algorithmic  perspective to  is really being driven by what people  need  this is this is great advice and it's  been such a fabulous conversation i  i hate to have to cut it off but i'm  afraid that is all the time we have for  today  so thanks so much to all of you all of  our panelists for sharing their insights  and experience  experiences we're really thrilled to  have you as part of the canada cfr ai  chairs program  and thanks to the audience for for great  questions and discussion over the chat  this has been  fantastic  you
 

